From f735d42754e7fce260bc84b037f7106c397da7da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <services.claude@accounts.ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 12:13:12 -0700
Subject: [DOSKUTSU] patch 0296 -- centre the 320x240 screen in an oversized
 surface

Some VBE implementations cannot offer a mode as small as the engine's
logical screen. The ATI Mach64-CT is the found case: the chip has no
double scanning, and 320x200 / 320x240 are double-scanned modes, so its
smallest mode is 512x384 and no driver can synthesise a smaller one
(SciTech UniVBE 6.70 says so by name when it loads, ATI's own M64VBE
reports "320 modes enabled" and still produces none, and the card's VBE
mode attributes clear bit 8 where the ViRGE and Cirrus both set it).
SDL correctly picks the closest mode and hands the engine a 512x384
surface for a 320x240 game.

Two coordinate systems then fought over that surface. Everything routed
through SDL_Renderer was scaled up by the LETTERBOX logical presentation
-- 512x384 and 640x480 both match 4:3 exactly, so SDL takes its STRETCH
branch and fills the surface -- while drawTileFast, the one direct-to-
window writer with no dimension gate, kept writing 1:1 at the origin.
That is the Mach64 photographs: a scaled backdrop and sprite layer with
an unscaled tilemap pinned to the top-left corner, and glyphs shredded
because the partial-flush rects were logical-space while the pixels they
were meant to push were not.

Keep one coordinate system instead. The engine goes on drawing a 1:1
320x240 image and that image is placed at a fixed offset inside the
larger surface:

  - initVideo disables the logical presentation and installs a render
    viewport at the offset. The software renderer applies a viewport as
    both a translation and a surface clip rect, so a draw that hangs off
    the logical screen is clipped rather than bleeding into the margin.
  - drawTileFast clips to the logical box, carrying its source rect, then
    translates by the same offset. It clamps against the destination
    SURFACE, so translating without clipping would spill tiles that hang
    off the logical right or bottom edge into the margin.
  - _record_dirty_rect translates after its logical clip, so a partial
    flush addresses the region that was actually drawn.
  - the margin is painted once per window surface, directly on the
    surface so the viewport does not clip the fill, and a full refresh is
    forced so it reaches VRAM.

Centring at 1:1 rather than scaling into the native mode is deliberate:
it costs no scaling work on a machine that has none to spare, it needs
no per-path scale awareness, and 512x384 is 196 KB/frame against
640x480's ~300 KB plus the doubling cost.

Keeping the per-frame flush the size of the live region needed three
more changes, none of which came for free from the existing dirty-rect
machinery:

  - direct-VESA stands down. It is a full-surface present --
    SDL_DOSVesaDirectPresentFull pushes fb_size and SDL/0034 has no rect
    variant -- and it was taking 97 of every 100 flips, so it owned the
    steady state and bypassed dirty-rect tracking entirely.
  - the flush bbox is intersected with the box. The prev-frame diff
    reports changed regions as full-surface-width bands, so the bbox
    would have pushed 512-wide rows for a 320-wide picture.
  - the 75% full-flush threshold is suppressed. It trades a rect flush
    for one contiguous blast once most of the screen has changed, which
    is right only while the surface IS the logical screen. Oversized, a
    full present pushes the whole surface to update the box, and the
    margin cannot have changed.

Measured under DOSBox-X with low-resolution VESA modes disabled (a
640x480 stand-in for the Mach64's 512x384): steady state is
dirty_path_taken=100 / full_flush_taken=0 and the engine reports
`flush rect=320x240@160,120 bytes=76800 (surface would be 640x480
bytes=307200)`.

Every touched site is guarded by _dos_center_active, which is set only
when the surface is larger than the logical screen. On a card that does
offer 320x240 the offset is zero, the guard is false, and each site
keeps its pre-patch behaviour -- which matters because the banked
benchmark matrix was measured on that path. Verified against a pre-patch
build at native 320x240: byte-identical Options screen, identical
drawcall totals across 127 matched flip indices, fast-blit path still
100% engaged, logical presentation still LETTERBOX.

Killswitch SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_CENTER_OVERSIZED=0 restores the scaled
logical-presentation path.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
